# Kapuletu Admin Panel Architecture & Implementation Plan

This document outlines the proposed implementation plan for the Kapuletu Platform Admin Panel, based on a deep-dive audit of the current codebase infrastructure.

## 1. Goal Description

The objective is to provide Kapuletu administrators with a professional, end-to-end, and fully functional admin panel. This panel will enable platform supervision, user management, intelligence analytics, logs auditing, financial tracking, and system configuration. 

### Audit Findings
- **Backend Status:** The `kapuletu-backend` already has a heavily structured and well-defined admin API located at `services/admin/router.py`. It includes comprehensive modules for:
  - **Module A:** Platform Intelligence (`AnalyticsService`)
  - **Module B:** User Lifecycle & Support (`UserService`)
  - **Module C:** AI Parser Governance (`AIGovernanceService`)
  - **Module D:** Subscription Revenue & Plans (`FinanceService`)
  - **Module E:** CRM & System Communications (`CRMService`)
  - **Module F:** Forensic Audit & Forensics (`AuditService`)
- **Frontend Status:** The `kapuletu-frontend` (Next.js application) has the beginnings of an admin interface under `src/app/(protected)/admin` with routes for `audit`, `groups`, and `profile`. However, it lacks the full suite of UI modules to consume the powerful backend APIs.
- **Codebase Strategy:** We **DO NOT need another codebase**. The most efficient, scalable, and maintainable approach is to build the Admin Panel within the existing `kapuletu-frontend` Next.js application, extending the `(protected)/admin` route structure. This ensures we share UI components, styling (Tailwind/CSS), and authentication logic seamlessly.

> [!TIP]
> Building the admin panel in the existing Next.js frontend minimizes technical debt, avoids cross-origin request issues, and centralizes deployment.

